      "source": [
        "This satisfies $\\mathbf{1}_{\\bar{B}_{R/2}} \\le \\psi_R \\le \\mathbf{1}_{\\bar{B}_R}$.\n",
        "Integrating gives:\n",
        "$$\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} \\psi_R \\, d\\mu_n \\le \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} \\mathbf{1}_{\\bar{B}_R} \\, d\\mu_n = \\mu_n(\\bar{B}_R)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Taking $\\liminf_{n \\to \\infty}$ on both sides:\n",
        "$$\\liminf_{n \\to \\infty} \\mu_n(\\bar{B}_R) \\ge \\liminf_{n \\to \\infty} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} \\psi_R \\, d\\mu_n = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} \\psi_R \\, d\\mu \\ge \\mu(\\bar{B}_{R/2})$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since $\\mu(\\bar{B}_{R/2}) \\uparrow 1$ as $R \\to \\infty$, for any $\\alpha \\in (0,1)$, we can choose $R$ large enough such that $\\mu(\\bar{B}_{R/2}) > \\alpha$.\n",
        "Thus, $\\liminf_{n \\to \\infty} \\mu_n(\\bar{B}_R) \\ge \\alpha$, which implies $\\lim_{R \\to \\infty} \\inf_n \\mu_n(\\bar{B}_R) = 1$. \n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#thm-cc-convergence}\n",
        "## $C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ Characterization\n",
        "Let $\\mu_n, \\mu \\in \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^d, \\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^d))$. Then $\\mu_n \\implies \\mu$ if and only if:\n",
        "$$\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} f \\, d\\mu_n \\longrightarrow \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} f \\, d\\mu \\quad \\forall f \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$$\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "## Proof of @thm-cc-convergence\n",
        "\n",
        "- $(\\implies)$: Immediate since $C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d) \\subseteq C_b(\\mathbb{R}^d)$.\n",
        "- $(\\impliedby)$: Let $f \\in C_b(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ with $\\|f\\|_\\infty < \\infty$. Truncate $f$ using the cutoff function $\\psi_R \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ defined in @lem-tightness-balls:\n",
        "  $$f_R(x) = f(x) \\cdot \\psi_R(x) \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "  We decompose the integration error using a three-term triangle inequality:\n",
        "  $$\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "  \\left| \\int f d\\mu - \\int f d\\mu_n \\right| &\\le \\left| \\int f d\\mu - \\int f_R d\\mu \\right| + \\left| \\int f_R d\\mu - \\int f_R d\\mu_n \\right| \\\\\n",
        "  &\\quad + \\left| \\int f_R d\\mu_n - \\int f d\\mu_n \\right|\n",
        "  \\end{aligned}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "  Taking $\\limsup_{n \\to \\infty}$ on both sides:\n",
        "  $$\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "  \\limsup_{n\\to\\infty} \\left| \\int f d\\mu - \\int f d\\mu_n \\right| &\\le \\int |f - f_R| \\, d\\mu + 0 + \\limsup_{n\\to\\infty} \\int |f - f_R| \\, d\\mu_n\n",
        "  \\end{aligned}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "  For the tail integrals:\n",
        "  $$\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} |f - f_R| \\, d\\mu_n \\le \\|f\\|_\\infty \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} (1 - \\psi_R) \\, d\\mu_n \\le \\|f\\|_\\infty \\left( 1 - \\mu_n(\\bar{B}_{R/2}) \\right)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "  Taking $R \\to \\infty$, @lem-tightness-balls guarantees $\\sup_n (1 - \\mu_n(\\bar{B}_{R/2})) \\to 0$. Thus:\n",
        "  $$\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty} \\left| \\int f d\\mu - \\int f d\\mu_n \\right| \\xrightarrow{R\\to\\infty} 0$$\n",
        "  Hence $\\int f d\\mu_n \\to \\int f d\\mu$ for all $f \\in C_b(\\mathbb{R}^d)$. \n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Weak Convergence via Smooth Test Functions\n",
        "\n",
        "We can further reduce the test function class to smooth functions with compact support, $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d) = \\{ f \\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d) : \\text{supp}(f) \\subseteq K \\text{ for some compact } K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^d \\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#cor-smooth-convergence}\n",
        "### Corollary: $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ Characterization\n",
        "Let $\\mu_n, \\mu \\in \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R}^d, \\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^d))$. Then $\\mu_n \\implies \\mu$ if and only if:\n",
        "$$\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} f \\, d\\mu_n \\longrightarrow \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} f \\, d\\mu \\quad \\forall f \\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "## Proof of @cor-smooth-convergence\n",
        "\n",
        "- $(\\implies)$: Since $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d) \\subseteq C_b(\\mathbb{R}^d)$, this direction is immediate from the definition of weak convergence.\n",
        "- $(\\impliedby)$: By @thm-cc-convergence, it suffices to show that convergence over $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ implies convergence over $C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Fix $f \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ supported on a compact set $K \\subset \\mathbb{R}^d$. Let $\\rho_\\epsilon(x) = \\epsilon^{-d} \\rho(x/\\epsilon)$ be a standard smooth mollifier with $\\text{supp}(\\rho_\\epsilon) \\subseteq B_\\epsilon(0)$ and $\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} \\rho_\\epsilon(u) \\, du = 1$.\n",
        "Define the mollified function:\n",
        "$$f_\\epsilon(x) = (f * \\rho_\\epsilon)(x) = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} f(x - u) \\rho_\\epsilon(u) \\, du = \\int_{B_\\epsilon(0)} f(x - u) \\rho_\\epsilon(u) \\, du$$\n",
        "\n",
        "- $f_\\epsilon \\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ with support contained in $K + \\bar{B}_\\epsilon$.\n",
        "- Since $f \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ has compact support, it is uniformly continuous on $\\mathbb{R}^d$. Therefore:\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  \\begin{align*}\n",
        "  \\|f - f_\\epsilon\\|_\\infty &= \\sup_{x \\in \\mathbb{R}^d} \\left| \\int_{B_\\epsilon(0)} (f(x) - f(x-u)) \\rho_\\epsilon(u) \\, du \\right|\\\\\n",
        "  &\\le \\sup_{x \\in \\mathbb{R}^d} \\int_{B_\\epsilon(0)} |f(x) - f(x-u)| \\, \\rho_\\epsilon(u) \\, du \\\\\n",
        "  &\\le \\left(\\sup_{x \\in \\mathbb{R}^d} \\sup_{|u| \\le \\epsilon} |f(x) - f(x-u)|\\right) \\underbrace{\\int_{B_\\epsilon(0)} \\rho_\\epsilon(u) \\, du}_{=1} \\xrightarrow{\\epsilon \\to 0} 0\n",
        "  \\end{align*}\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  by uniform continuity of $f$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Using a three-term inequality, and noting that $\\mu_n, \\mu$ are probability measures so $\\int |g| \\, d\\nu \\le \\|g\\|_\\infty$ for any probability measure $\\nu$:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "\\left| \\int f \\, d\\mu_n - \\int f \\, d\\mu \\right| &\\le \\left| \\int f \\, d\\mu_n - \\int f_\\epsilon \\, d\\mu_n \\right| + \\left| \\int f_\\epsilon \\, d\\mu_n - \\int f_\\epsilon \\, d\\mu \\right| \\\\\n",
        "&\\quad + \\left| \\int f_\\epsilon \\, d\\mu - \\int f \\, d\\mu \\right| \\\\\n",
        "&\\le \\|f - f_\\epsilon\\|_\\infty + \\left| \\int f_\\epsilon \\, d\\mu_n - \\int f_\\epsilon \\, d\\mu \\right| + \\|f - f_\\epsilon\\|_\\infty.\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Taking $\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}$: since $f_\\epsilon \\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ and convergence over $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ is assumed, the middle term vanishes. Thus:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty} \\left| \\int f \\, d\\mu_n - \\int f \\, d\\mu \\right| \\le 2 \\|f - f_\\epsilon\\|_\\infty.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Sending $\\epsilon \\to 0$ makes the right-hand side arbitrarily small, proving $\\int f \\, d\\mu_n \\to \\int f \\, d\\mu$ for all $f \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$. Hence $\\mu_n \\implies \\mu$ by @thm-cc-convergence. \n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Convergence of Cumulative Distribution Functions (CDFs) on $\\mathbb{R}$\n",
        "\n",
        "Every probability measure $\\mu$ on $(\\mathbb{R}, \\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}))$ uniquely corresponds to a right-continuous cumulative distribution function (CDF):\n",
        "$$F(t) = \\mu((-\\infty, t])$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For a function $F: S \\to T$, let $\\text{Cont}(F)$ denote the set of continuity points of $F$, and $\\text{Disc}(F) = S \\setminus \\text{Cont}(F)$ denote its discontinuity points.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#exm-dirac-cdf}\n",
        "## CDF Convergence Failure at Discontinuity Points\n",
        "Let $a_n = a + \\frac{1}{n} \\to a$ in $\\mathbb{R}$. The corresponding measures are $\\mu_n = \\delta_{a_n}$ and $\\mu = \\delta_a$.\n",
        "Their CDFs are:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_n(t) = \\delta_{a_n}((-\\infty, t]) = \n",
        "\\begin{cases} \n",
        "1, & t \\ge a_n \\\\ \n",
        "0, & t < a_n \n",
        "\\end{cases} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad F(t) = \n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        " 1, & t \\ge a \\\\\n",
        " 0, & t < a \n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Evaluating at $t = a$:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $F_n(a) = \\mathbf{1}_{\\{a_n \\le a\\}} = 0$ for all $n \\ge 1$.\n",
        "- $F(a) = \\mathbf{1}_{\\{a \\le a\\}} = 1$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus $F_n(a) \\to 0 \\neq F(a) = 1$.\n",
        "However, $t = a$ is a discontinuity point of $F$ ($a \\notin \\text{Cont}(F)$).\n",
        "For all $t \\neq a$, $F_n(t) \\to F(t)$ holds.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#thm-cdf-convergence}\n",
        "## CDF Characterization of Weak Convergence on $\\mathbb{R}$\n",
        "Let $\\mu_n, \\mu \\in \\mathcal{P}(\\mathbb{R})$ with CDFs $F_n(t) = \\mu_n((-\\infty, t])$ and $F(t) = \\mu((-\\infty, t])$.\n",
        "Then $\\mu_n \\implies \\mu$ if and only if:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_n(t) \\longrightarrow F(t) \\quad \\forall t \\in \\text{Cont}(F).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "## Proof of @thm-cdf-convergence\n",
        "\n",
        "- $(\\implies)$:\n",
        "  Let $t \\in \\text{Cont}(F)$. Then $\\mu(\\{t\\}) = F(t) - F(t^-) = 0$.\n",
        "  The boundary of $A = (-\\infty, t]$ is $\\partial A = \\{t\\}$.\n",
        "  Since $\\mu(\\partial A) = \\mu(\\{t\\}) = 0$, $A$ is a $\\mu$-continuity set.\n",
        "  By Portmanteau Theorem (Condition 5):\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  F_n(t) = \\mu_n((-\\infty, t]) \\longrightarrow \\mu((-\\infty, t]) = F(t).\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "\n",
        "- $(\\impliedby)$:\n",
        "  Since $F$ is a non-decreasing function on $\\mathbb{R}$, its set of discontinuities $\\text{Disc}(F)$ is at most countable. Thus $\\text{Cont}(F)$ is dense in $\\mathbb{R}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "  Let $f \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R})$ with support $\\text{supp}(f) \\subseteq [a, b]$.\n",
        "  Since $f$ is continuous on a compact interval, it is **uniformly continuous**.\n",
        "  Fix $\\epsilon > 0$. Choose $\\delta > 0$ such that $|x - y| < \\delta \\implies |f(x) - f(y)| < \\epsilon/2$.\n",
        "\n",
        "  Construct a partition $\\pi = \\{x_0 < x_1 < \\dots < x_k\\}$ of $[a,b]$ such that:\n",
        "\n",
        "  1. $x_j \\in \\text{Cont}(F)$ for all $j = 0, 1, \\dots, k$.\n",
        "  2. $|x_j - x_{j-1}| < \\delta$ for all $j = 1, \\dots, k$.\n",
        "\n",
        "  Define a step function approximation $f_\\pi$:\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  f_\\pi(x) = \\sum_{j=1}^k f(x_j) \\mathbf{1}_{(x_{j-1}, x_j]}(x).\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "\n",
        "  By uniform continuity, $\\|f - f_\\pi\\|_\\infty < \\epsilon/2$.\n",
        "  Therefore, for any probability measure $\\nu$:\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  \\left| \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} f \\, d\\nu - \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} f_\\pi \\, d\\nu \\right| \\le \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} |f - f_\\pi| \\, d\\nu \\le \\frac{\\epsilon}{2}.\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "\n",
        "  Now evaluate $\\int f_\\pi \\, d\\mu_n$:\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} f_\\pi \\, d\\mu_n = \\sum_{j=1}^k f(x_j) \\mu_n((x_{j-1}, x_j]) = \\sum_{j=1}^k f(x_j) \\big( F_n(x_j) - F_n(x_{j-1}) \\big).\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "\n",
        "  Since $x_j \\in \\text{Cont}(F)$ for all $j$, $F_n(x_j) \\to F(x_j)$ as $n \\to \\infty$. Thus:\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} f_\\pi \\, d\\mu_n \\xrightarrow{n\\to\\infty} \\sum_{j=1}^k f(x_j) \\big( F(x_j) - F(x_{j-1}) \\big) = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}} f_\\pi \\, d\\mu.\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "\n",
        "  Finally, using the triangle inequality:\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  \\begin{aligned}\n",
        "  \\left| \\int f d\\mu_n - \\int f d\\mu \\right| &\\le \\left| \\int f d\\mu_n - \\int f_\\pi d\\mu_n \\right| + \\left| \\int f_\\pi d\\mu_n - \\int f_\\pi d\\mu \\right| \\\\\n",
        "  &\\quad + \\left| \\int f_\\pi d\\mu - \\int f d\\mu \\right| \\\\\n",
        "  &\\le \\frac{\\epsilon}{2} + \\left| \\int f_\\pi d\\mu_n - \\int f_\\pi d\\mu \\right| + \\frac{\\epsilon}{2}.\n",
        "  \\end{aligned}\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "\n",
        "  Taking $\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}$:\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  \\limsup_{n\\to\\infty} \\left| \\int f d\\mu_n - \\int f d\\mu \\right| \\le \\epsilon.\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "\n",
        "  Since $\\epsilon > 0$ was arbitrary, $\\int f d\\mu_n \\to \\int f d\\mu$ for all $f \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R})$. By Theorem 2.2, $\\mu_n \\implies \\mu$. \n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Takeaways\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Smaller test-function classes suffice on $\\mathbb{R}^d$.**\n",
        "   On $\\mathbb{R}^d$, weak convergence $\\mu_n \\implies \\mu$ is equivalent to $\\int f \\, d\\mu_n \\to \\int f \\, d\\mu$ for all $f$ in any of the following nested classes:\n",
        "   $$C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d) \\subsetneq C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d) \\subsetneq \\text{Lip}_b(\\mathbb{R}^d) \\subsetneq C_b(\\mathbb{R}^d).$$\n",
        "   The gain is that $C_c$ and $C_c^\\infty$ consist of compactly supported functions, which are analytically more tractable.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. **Tightness underlies the reduction from $C_b$ to $C_c$.**\n",
        "   By @lem-tightness-balls, weak convergence forces $\\lim_{R\\to\\infty} \\inf_n \\mu_n(\\bar{B}_R) = 1$: the sequence concentrates uniformly on large balls. This allows tail contributions to be controlled by $\\|f\\|_\\infty \\cdot \\sup_n (1 - \\mu_n(\\bar{B}_{R/2})) \\to 0$ as $R \\to \\infty$.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. **Mollification gives the further reduction from $C_c$ to $C_c^\\infty$.**\n",
        "   Every $f \\in C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ is approximated uniformly by $f_\\epsilon = f * \\rho_\\epsilon \\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$, with $\\|f - f_\\epsilon\\|_\\infty \\to 0$ as $\\epsilon \\to 0$ by uniform continuity of $f$. This reduction is essential for Fourier-analytic methods (e.g., characteristic functions), which test against smooth oscillatory functions.\n",
        "\n",
        "4. **On $\\mathbb{R}$, weak convergence is equivalent to pointwise CDF convergence at continuity points.**\n",
        "   By @thm-cdf-convergence, $\\mu_n \\implies \\mu$ if and only if $F_n(t) \\to F(t)$ for all $t \\in \\text{Cont}(F)$. Convergence can fail at discontinuity points of $F$, as shown in @exm-dirac-cdf with $\\delta_{a+1/n} \\implies \\delta_a$. Since any monotone $F$ has at most countably many discontinuities, the exceptional set excluded from the convergence requirement is at most countable, and $\\text{Cont}(F)$ is dense in $\\mathbb{R}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "5. **The full Portmanteau equivalence on $\\mathbb{R}^d$ now has eight conditions.**\n",
        "   This lecture adds three items to the five Portmanteau conditions from Lecture 22.1: testing against $C_c(\\mathbb{R}^d)$, testing against $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$, and (in dimension $d = 1$) CDF convergence at all continuity points of the limit. Each is strictly more convenient to verify than testing all of $C_b(\\mathbb{R}^d)$."
